  ---
  Q1 — What does "信息密度 / Density" actually change?

  PRD §5.4 lists 信息密度 as a top-level toolbar control alongside mode and
  theme. ARCHITECTURE §4.5 declares Density = 'comfortable' | 'compact' | 
  'dense' inside CompileOptions (i.e. compiler sees it). But neither doc says 
  what each value does to the output. This matters because:

  - If density only changes spacing/font-size/line-height → it's CSS, belongs in
   ThemeTokens, doesn't deserve toolbar-level prominence next to mode/theme.
  - If density changes structure (e.g. Brief at dense merges cards 2-by-2; Deck
  at dense allows two slides per "page"; Reader at dense drops the TOC) → it's
  compiler-level, lives in RenderPlan, and toolbar promotion is justified.
  - If it does both → we need to say which dimensions live where, or density
  quietly becomes the most powerful and least-documented knob in the product.

  My recommendation: density is compiler-level AND theme-level, with a clear
  split.

  - Compiler-level (changes RenderPlan shape): comfortable shows full evidence
  sections; compact collapses them into accordions; dense strips appendix and
  merges card grids. The compiler decides which RenderNodes exist.
  - Theme-level (CSS only): each density value also maps to a spacing/typography
   scale token set, so the same RenderPlan looks tighter or airier.
  - This justifies its toolbar slot (it has structural consequences, not just
  cosmetic) but keeps theme tokens pure visual.

  Does this match your intent — or did you mean density to be purely visual (a
  theme sub-axis), or purely structural (a per-mode variant)?

